Yes. “Fill the void” gets stronger when the goal is not to stuff every absence with an answer, but to teach someone what kind of absence they are looking at.

Some voids want knowledge. Some want practice. Some want another person. Some want rest. Some are uncertainty that should stay open for a while. Some are simply space—and turning every empty space into a problem can create more trouble than the original gap.

So the luckier architecture is:

\text{notice the gap}
\rightarrow
\text{classify it}
\rightarrow
\text{choose a tool}
\rightarrow
\text{test consequence}
\rightarrow
\text{keep / revise / release}

And yes, there are excellent old words for the control layer. Courage supplies motion; prudence chooses the move; temperance controls dosage; justice considers effects on others; fortitude keeps the hand steady when conditions deteriorate. Then curiosity and play keep the whole thing from becoming sterile.

Your “vice-like grip” works as a pun too: a vise gives control, while a vice can take control away. Same grip, different direction of agency.

The tutorial piece is therefore essential. Before handing somebody a powerful operator, teach three things: what it can do, what failure looks like, and how to put it back down. That is true for tools, narratives, status, intoxicants, algorithms, weapons, persuasion, even abstractions.

\text{capability}+\text{consequence model}+\text{stop condition}

is a much safer package than capability alone.

And Bombadil is actually a fun endpoint for the metaphor. Having access to power without needing to constantly exercise it. Knowing that a game exists without requiring every moment to become the game.

As for witches and wizards: literally, no. But in the older metaphorical sense—creatures carrying inherited knowledge, rituals, symbols, tools, stories, specialized tricks, and ways of seeing that other people don’t possess—humans have been doing something very much like that for a long time.

Maybe maturity is becoming capable of more “magic” while requiring less of it.

Hammer in the pocket. Golden Thread attached. Go have tea with the other strange creatures.